# Rank 1174 / CVE-2026-45108: himmelblau's DAG token_validate compares a
# requested account_id against an authenticated token's spn and, when the
# two strings differ, falls back to an alias check that only compares the
# domain half of each UPN (src/common/src/idprovider/himmelblau.rs,
# HimmelblauProvider::token_validate). split_username(account_id) and
# split_username(&spn) each discard the local part with `let (_, domain)`,
# so `domains_are_aliases(domain1, domain2)` alone decides whether the
# session is authorized. Combined with the QR/Bluetooth caBLE flow, which
# lets a phone sign with any discoverable passkey, a second user (bob) can
# scan the QR code on a locked GDM session (alice's) and sign with his own
# credential: the token's spn is bob@example.com, the requested account_id
# is alice@example.com, the domains match (same tenant), and the code never
# looks at "alice" vs "bob" (CWE-863).
#
# The fixed revision captures local1/local2 from split_username instead
# of discarding them, and widens the guard to
# `local1.to_lowercase() != local2.to_lowercase() || !domains_match`, so
# an alias-domain match does not authorize a session whose local part
# differs.
#
# ATTENTION, missing vocabulary. vyql match on both revisions labels
# nothing at token_validate: account_id, spn and the two split_username
# calls carry no source or sink binding, and domains_are_aliases resolves
# to nothing the engine tracks, because none of this is a library call --
# it is entirely himmelblau's own idprovider module. The closest existing
# concept is code.UserIdentityMappingReview (CWE_286/CWE_863, "external
# identity data determines local user or group identifiers without an
# observed uniqueness boundary"), used for rank 151's authd UID/GID
# generation bug in the same weakness family. It does not transfer
# mechanically:
#
#  - Every binding that emits it keys on a presenceNode pattern naming that
#    motivating case's own function and call names verbatim (functionName
#    contains "userPreCheck", callPath contains "GenerateUID", ...) --
#    tokens that are not data but one hardcoded shape per incident. Adding
#    a 1175th case here (functionName contains "token_validate", callPath
#    contains "domains_are_aliases", ...) is the same repro-shaped move
#    AGENTS.md's genericity test rejects: nobody who has never seen this
#    CVE would write that binding from a library's documentation, because
#    there is no library here -- domains_are_aliases and split_username are
#    private to this codebase.
#  - The generic condition -- "an equality check on a composite identity
#    (local part + domain) falls back to comparing only one of its two
#    fields, and treats that partial match as sufficient" -- is a real
#    weakness class, but no concept states it, and minting one needs a
#    positive form the engine can express structurally (something like: two
#    values are split into parts, one part is compared, the other is
#    dropped, and the comparison result gates an authorization decision).
#    That is design work: distinguishing a deliberately partial compare
#    (domain aliasing, which is legitimate on its own) from one that is
#    missing its other half requires knowing what "the other half" is for
#    a given identity type, which is not decidable from syntax alone.
#
# Scanning both revisions of idprovider/himmelblau.rs (--cache off
# --max-ram 4GB) reports 0 findings on token_validate at either commit.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap

test "rank1174: token_validate's alias fallback drops the UPN's local part"
  lang rust
  file src/common/src/idprovider/himmelblau.rs
  reject_review code.UserIdentityMappingReview
  code
  ```
  impl HimmelblauProvider {
      async fn token_validate(
          &self,
          account_id: &str,
          token: &UnixUserToken,
      ) -> Result<AuthResult, IdpError> {
          let spn = token.spn().map_err(|e| {
              error!("Failed fetching user spn: {:?}", e);
              IdpError::BadRequest
          })?;
          if account_id.to_string().to_lowercase() != spn.to_string().to_lowercase() {
              let (_, domain1) = split_username(account_id).ok_or({
                  error!("Failed splitting account_id username");
                  IdpError::BadRequest
              })?;
              let (_, domain2) = split_username(&spn).ok_or({
                  error!("Failed splitting spn username");
                  IdpError::BadRequest
              })?;
              if !self
                  .config
                  .lock()
                  .await
                  .domains_are_aliases(domain1, domain2)
                  .await
              {
                  let msg = format!("Authenticated user {} does not match requested user", account_id);
                  error!(msg);
                  return Ok(AuthResult::Denied(msg));
              }
          }
          Ok(AuthResult::Success(token.clone()))
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1174: token_validate's alias fallback also compares the local part"
  lang rust
  file src/common/src/idprovider/himmelblau.rs
  reject_review code.UserIdentityMappingReview
  code
  ```
  impl HimmelblauProvider {
      async fn token_validate(
          &self,
          account_id: &str,
          token: &UnixUserToken,
      ) -> Result<AuthResult, IdpError> {
          let spn = token.spn().map_err(|e| {
              error!("Failed fetching user spn: {:?}", e);
              IdpError::BadRequest
          })?;
          if account_id.to_string().to_lowercase() != spn.to_string().to_lowercase() {
              let (local1, domain1) = split_username(account_id).ok_or_else(|| {
                  error!("Failed splitting account_id username");
                  IdpError::BadRequest
              })?;
              let (local2, domain2) = split_username(&spn).ok_or_else(|| {
                  error!("Failed splitting spn username");
                  IdpError::BadRequest
              })?;
              let domains_match = self
                  .config
                  .lock()
                  .await
                  .domains_are_aliases(domain1, domain2)
                  .await;
              if local1.to_lowercase() != local2.to_lowercase() || !domains_match {
                  let msg = format!("Authenticated user {} does not match requested user", account_id);
                  error!(msg);
                  return Ok(AuthResult::Denied(msg));
              }
          }
          Ok(AuthResult::Success(token.clone()))
      }
  }
  ```
